WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.510
You are fluent in a foreign language, when you know at least 20.000 words.

00:00:03.794 --> 00:00:04.446
Oh..

00:00:04.693 --> 00:00:08.629
You are fluent in a foreign language when you can tackle any topic at the drop of a hat

00:00:08.629 --> 00:00:10.048
Ehm..

00:00:10.048 --> 00:00:12.769
You are fluent in a language when you can speak a language perfectly.

00:00:12.769 --> 00:00:13.870
Aaaah..

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:16.147
Hi, my name is Luca, from Lucalampariello.com

00:00:16.147 --> 00:00:17.628
and today I am going to tackle

00:00:17.628 --> 00:00:22.759
the beautiful and much debated concept of fluency in a foreign language.

00:00:22.759 --> 00:00:23.959
Let's get started.

00:00:27.609 --> 00:00:29.879
It is astounding and quite surprising to see

00:00:30.012 --> 00:00:33.881
how differently people see a single word in the English language,

00:00:33.881 --> 00:00:35.451
and that is fluency.

00:00:35.451 --> 00:00:37.090
For a lot of people fluency

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:40.253
is strictly connected with the amount of words

00:00:40.253 --> 00:00:42.592
that you know in a language.

00:00:42.592 --> 00:00:43.901
So, some people think that

00:00:43.901 --> 00:00:46.482
the more words you know, the more fluent you are.

00:00:46.482 --> 00:00:50.111
But things are a little bit more nuanced than that.

00:00:50.111 --> 00:00:53.345
There are 2 main points I wanted to address in this regard.

00:00:53.345 --> 00:00:55.920
First of all, native speakers and non native speakers

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.922
tend to use just a small fraction

00:00:58.922 --> 00:01:01.801
of the existing vocabulary in a language.

00:01:01.801 --> 00:01:04.024
So the first point I wanted to address is that

00:01:04.024 --> 00:01:07.461
non native as well as native speakers of a specific language

00:01:07.461 --> 00:01:09.901
actually use just a small portion

00:01:09.901 --> 00:01:14.657
of the existing vocabulary in any language.

00:01:14.657 --> 00:01:16.823
Now, you might be wondering why and how

00:01:16.823 --> 00:01:19.508
it is possible that native and non native speakers

00:01:19.508 --> 00:01:24.221
use just a small fraction of the existing vocabulary in a foreign language.

00:01:24.221 --> 00:01:25.362
For example, are we talking about

00:01:25.362 --> 00:01:28.560
dozens, hundreds or thousands of words?

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:29.441
The reality is

00:01:29.441 --> 00:01:30.515
it does not really matter,

00:01:30.515 --> 00:01:32.090
but what matters is that

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:35.301
we tend to use a small fraction of the existing words

00:01:35.301 --> 00:01:38.240
because we work in NETWORKS.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:41.085
So, if you know, say, 1000 words,

00:01:41.085 --> 00:01:43.692
you can come up with hundreds or thousands

00:01:43.692 --> 00:01:45.520
of possible combinations,

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:46.707
that is, sentences,

00:01:46.707 --> 00:01:47.951
to get your point across.

00:01:47.951 --> 00:01:51.656
So, for example, if I conjure up the word "heart"

00:01:51.656 --> 00:01:55.961
you might have in your head - if you are a native English speaker -

00:01:55.961 --> 00:01:59.491
hundreds..maybe not hundreds but dozens of expressions

00:01:59.491 --> 00:02:01.853
related to the word "heart",

00:02:01.853 --> 00:02:03.221
which is a common word.

00:02:03.221 --> 00:02:07.092
For example "from the bottom of my heart"

00:02:07.092 --> 00:02:08.762
"This is not for the fainted hearted",

00:02:08.762 --> 00:02:10.432
"at the heart of the matter"

00:02:10.432 --> 00:02:11.393
"Heart of gold"

00:02:11.393 --> 00:02:12.497
"Heart of stone"

00:02:12.497 --> 00:02:18.041
So, as you can see - and this goes for a lot of common and uncommon words -

00:02:18.041 --> 00:02:20.391
there is always a constellation of elements

00:02:20.391 --> 00:02:22.171
surrounding that word.

00:02:22.171 --> 00:02:24.132
And what happens is that native speakers,

00:02:24.132 --> 00:02:27.080
and you can see it in a 5 or 6 year old kid -

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:28.823
don't know that many words, but

00:02:28.823 --> 00:02:31.851
they can arrange them like (adult) native spekers would,

00:02:31.851 --> 00:02:34.211
so forming sentences and expressions

00:02:34.211 --> 00:02:36.759
that are commonly used in a foreign language.

00:02:36.759 --> 00:02:39.781
That means being fluent in a language.

00:02:42.615 --> 00:02:44.607
Some years ago, when I was in Russia,

00:02:44.667 --> 00:02:48.499
the organizer of a conference I participated in and myself

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.709
decided to make a few short videos in Russian,

00:02:50.709 --> 00:02:53.249
and it turned out that, at a certain point,

00:02:53.249 --> 00:02:55.918
when we were shooting live, filming live,

00:02:55.918 --> 00:03:00.866
I could not remember the word "combination (mixture) of", that is "смесь" in Russian.

00:03:00.866 --> 00:03:03.467
And I remember that someone wrote in the comment section below

00:03:03.467 --> 00:03:04.988
- a very nice place -

00:03:04.988 --> 00:03:09.938
that I "didn't speak Russian well" or "fluently"

00:03:09.938 --> 00:03:12.461
because "I didn't know such a common word".

00:03:12.461 --> 00:03:14.102
So, going back to what I was saying before,

00:03:14.102 --> 00:03:17.796
this is not just a matter of knowing one word or not knowing one word,

00:03:17.796 --> 00:03:19.406
but the point here is that

00:03:19.406 --> 00:03:21.857
fluency is not a line that you cross,

00:03:21.857 --> 00:03:24.328
so either you are fluent or you are not fluent,

00:03:24.328 --> 00:03:28.188
there are a lot of different degrees of fluency

00:03:28.188 --> 00:03:30.017
because fluency at the end of the day

00:03:30.017 --> 00:03:31.641
is just a continuous line.

00:03:31.641 --> 00:03:35.947
What we do for the sake of simplicity is

00:03:35.947 --> 00:03:38.892
to distinguish 3 main phases of fluency,

00:03:38.892 --> 00:03:41.719
so there is a continous spectrum of fluency,

00:03:41.719 --> 00:03:43.370
but we mainly divide it into

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:46.892
elementary, intermediate and advanced,

00:03:46.892 --> 00:03:50.480
and each and everyone of these phases corresponds to different abilities,

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:53.196
and I am still referring to spoken fluency.

00:03:53.196 --> 00:03:55.367
And another thing that you have to remember is that

00:03:55.367 --> 00:03:58.389
fluency - the quality and the quantity of fluency -

00:03:58.389 --> 00:04:00.780
depends on your path.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:04.188
Native speakers have all the same path more or less,

00:04:04.188 --> 00:04:07.678
but the truth is, adults learn in different ways,

00:04:07.678 --> 00:04:11.159
so the channels, the people, the things you do, the materials

00:04:11.159 --> 00:04:14.467
they all have an impact on the degree,

00:04:14.467 --> 00:04:17.563
quality and quantity of fluency,

00:04:17.563 --> 00:04:19.867
so never forget that what you do

00:04:19.867 --> 00:04:21.780
impacts who you are.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:27.824
Now that I told you what fluency is NOT

00:04:27.917 --> 00:04:31.002
let me give you my own personal definition of fluency.

00:04:31.002 --> 00:04:33.692
To me, spoken fluency is

00:04:33.692 --> 00:04:36.949
the linguistic ability, combined with cultural awareness,

00:04:36.949 --> 00:04:39.771
to confidently and smoothly communicate

00:04:39.771 --> 00:04:42.245
with native speakers in a meaningful way.

00:04:42.245 --> 00:04:46.971
That querulous and fatuous roué enervated me to no end,

00:04:46.971 --> 00:04:51.460
at every party, he wanted to be the cynosure".

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:52.512
Well, first of all

00:04:52.512 --> 00:04:53.951
I hoped I pronounced it well,

00:04:53.951 --> 00:04:55.021
and second of all,

00:04:55.021 --> 00:04:56.692
what the heck did I just read?!

00:04:56.692 --> 00:04:57.720
As you can see,

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:02.542
these are actually words that exist in the English language -

00:05:02.542 --> 00:05:04.146
from querulous to fatuous,

00:05:04.146 --> 00:05:07.008
but they are less common, so they are less used.

00:05:07.008 --> 00:05:08.867
There are synonyms of these words,

00:05:08.867 --> 00:05:10.358
but how many times have you heard these words

00:05:10.358 --> 00:05:11.992
even if you are a native English speaker?

00:05:11.992 --> 00:05:14.256
That goes to show that

00:05:14.256 --> 00:05:16.693
fluency is a RELATIVE PHENOMENON,

00:05:16.693 --> 00:05:17.872
as I was saying before,

00:05:17.872 --> 00:05:20.010
we don't know and we can't use

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:23.255
all the existing words in a spoken language,

00:05:23.255 --> 00:05:25.971
there are words that are very uncommon,

00:05:25.971 --> 00:05:29.062
there are words that are used in specialized fields,

00:05:29.062 --> 00:05:31.371
and there even words that are common,

00:05:31.371 --> 00:05:34.419
but that have uncommon connotations of meaning.

00:05:34.419 --> 00:05:35.772
This goes to show that

00:05:35.772 --> 00:05:39.892
every language is a huge, immense ocean to explore.

00:05:39.892 --> 00:05:42.370
We will never explore all of it.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:44.882
Nobody knows a language perfectly.

00:05:44.882 --> 00:05:45.770
And that's fine!

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:47.602
The final and ultimate goal

00:05:47.602 --> 00:05:49.722
is always to communicate,

00:05:49.722 --> 00:05:52.861
so if you even know a tiny fraction of a language

00:05:52.861 --> 00:05:57.109
it is fine, as long as you can use that knowledge well,

00:05:57.109 --> 00:05:59.883
you can put it to use to communicate effectively.

00:05:59.883 --> 00:06:02.351
THAT is what fluency really is.

00:06:04.672 --> 00:06:05.662
So there you have it.

00:06:05.694 --> 00:06:06.417
In a nutshell,

00:06:06.417 --> 00:06:09.484
fluency is NOT just about the words that you know,

00:06:09.484 --> 00:06:11.719
but first and foremost how you use them.

00:06:11.719 --> 00:06:13.531
Fluency is not a line that you cross,

00:06:13.531 --> 00:06:14.699
so one day you are fluent

00:06:14.699 --> 00:06:16.859
and the other day you are not fluent or the other way around,

00:06:16.859 --> 00:06:18.798
but it is more like a continuous line

00:06:18.798 --> 00:06:21.636
that has no boundaries and

00:06:21.636 --> 00:06:23.459
it can mainly be divided into

00:06:23.459 --> 00:06:28.785
the three stages of elementary, intermediate and advanced fluency.

00:06:28.785 --> 00:06:31.938
And ultimately, fluency is a relative phenomenon,

00:06:31.938 --> 00:06:34.776
fluency means to use well your language

00:06:34.776 --> 00:06:37.036
in order to communicate with other human beings.

00:06:37.036 --> 00:06:40.386
It is not about knowing a language perfectly,

00:06:40.386 --> 00:06:41.737
because at the end of the day

00:06:41.737 --> 00:06:44.147
nobody, not even native speakers

00:06:44.147 --> 00:06:45.967
know their own language perfectly.

00:06:45.967 --> 00:06:47.448
So the bottom line of all this is that

00:06:47.448 --> 00:06:49.887
if you want to be truly fluent in a foreign language,

00:06:49.887 --> 00:06:53.446
you have to focus on the process of becoming fluent.

00:06:53.446 --> 00:06:57.207
You have to talk, read out loud, to read books

00:06:57.207 --> 00:06:59.315
to watch documentaries, movies,

00:06:59.315 --> 00:07:02.348
to go out with your friends, to live experiences..

00:07:02.348 --> 00:07:05.854
You have to make it happen in real life.

00:07:05.854 --> 00:07:09.417
That is what fluency is all about: living.

00:07:09.417 --> 00:07:11.277
So, as usual, if you want to know more

00:07:11.277 --> 00:07:13.709
please on the link in the description box

00:07:13.709 --> 00:07:16.286
there is a huge post waiting for you

00:07:16.286 --> 00:07:19.958
with a lot of practical steps that I did not include in this video,

00:07:19.958 --> 00:07:21.485
and hopefully I will see you there,

00:07:21.485 --> 00:07:24.577
feel free to drop comments and interact with me.

00:07:24.577 --> 00:07:27.576
Again, thanks a bunch for watching

00:07:27.576 --> 00:07:29.877
and see you very soon here on YouTube.

